# 0003. RunPod compute with operator-declared offerings and label-based reconciliation

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-11

Accepted by the owner on 2026-11-11: GPUs come only from offerings declared in config,
pods are created idempotently by name, and orphans are found from labels carried in the
pod's environment.

## Technical details
- **Problem**: GPU instances cost money by the hour. The platform must never provision
  hardware the operator didn't approve, and must find orphaned pods after a crash.
- **API**: RunPod REST v1 (`https://rest.runpod.io/v1`, `DefaultBaseURL`) through plain
  `net/http` in `adapters/outbound/compute/runpod`; RunPod types never leave the package.
  Key from `PERSONAL_AI_RUNPOD_API_KEY`.
- **Offerings**: declared in config under `compute.runpod.offerings` (`id`, `gpu_type_id`,
  `gpu_count`, `vram_per_gpu_gb`, `hourly_usd` for cost estimates), e.g. `a40-1x`,
  `a100-80g-1x`; never discovered from the API. Timeouts live under `deployment`
  (`provision_timeout` 15m, `ready_timeout` 30m, `poll_interval` 10s). `scheduling.max_active` caps running deployments
  (example config: 1) and so GPU spend; `scheduling.idle_timeout` (30m in the example) stops
  idle ones.
- **Idempotency**: RunPod has no idempotency keys. Every pod is named
  `<NamePrefix><idempotency key>` (prefix default `pai-`) and the name is looked up before
  `Provision` creates anything; the key is also stored as `PAI_IDEMPOTENCY_KEY` in the pod env.
- **Labels**: RunPod has no labels, so deployment labels travel as `PAI_LABEL_<KEY>`
  environment variables and are read back into `compute.Instance.Labels`. `ListInstances`
  only returns pods with the name prefix.
- **Reconciliation**: `deployment.Service.Reconcile` runs at startup (and an orphan check
  after an interrupted launch): it lists the prefixed pods, matches their `deployment_id`
  label to `deployments` rows, terminates pods no live deployment owns, and reports them in
  `ReconcileReport.OrphansTerminated`. No `compute_instances` table exists.
- **Consequences**: spending stays bounded by config; crash recovery needs no extra state. A
  new GPU type needs a config change and restart; the orphan sweep runs only at startup and
  on interrupted launches.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: RunPod REST API v1, Go `net/http`.
- Alternatives: discover offerings from the API: could provision unapproved, expensive GPUs;
  a `compute_instances` table: deferred until billing reconciliation needs it.
